    h2.twocol#how-can-i-use-paramojis-on-my-website How can I use paramojis on my website?
    div
      | The easiest way to link the image from <code>https://paramoji.org/paramoji.svg.php</code>.
      | You can set the request parameters for the paramoji dimensions from 0 to 100.
      | The request returns an SVG image.

    .code-container
      .code
        code
          span.first(class="xml")  &lt;img src=&quot;
          span(style="color:firebrick") https://paramoji.org/<wbr/>paramoji.svg.php
          span(style="color:gray") ?
          span(style="color:firebrick") v=70
          span(style="color:gray") &amp;
          span(style="color:firebrick") a=50
          span(style="color:gray") &amp;
          span(style="color:firebrick") d=45
          span(style="color:gray") &amp;
          span(style="color:firebrick") g=0
          span(style="color:gray") &amp;
          span(style="color:firebrick") c=0
          span(style="color:gray") &amp;
          span(style="color:firebrick") o=40
          span(class="xml") &quot;/&gt;

    .twocol
      table
        tr
          th Parameter
          th Description
        tr
          td <code>v</code>
          td Valence, aka pleasure, is the axis from bad to good, misery to pleasure, disagreement to agrement.
        tr
          td <code>a</code>
          td Arousal is the axis from calm to aroused, inactive to active, sleepy to alarmed.
        tr
          td <code>d</code>
          td Dominance is the axis from weakness to strength, fear to anger, submission to assertion.
        tr
          td <code>g</code>
          td Disgust is the axis from neutral to disgusted, repelled, sickened.
        tr
          td <code>c</code>
          td Contempt is the axis from neutral to smug, vain, disdainful and contemptful.
        tr
          td <code>o</code>
          td Control is the axis from uncontrolled to controlled, expressed to suppressed.
        tr
          td <code>b</code>
          td Blush from absent to maximum cheek blushing
        tr
          td <code>t</code>
          td Tears from absent to maximum tear size
        tr
          td <code>size</code>
          td Size of the generated image, default is 100%
        tr
          td <code>dark</code>
          td Dark mode, if set to 1, all lines are generated white to fit on black backgrounds.

    div
      p
        | The source code is licensed as MIT and can be accessed here:
      ul
        li
          a(href='paramoji.svg.php?showSource=true') PHP code
        li
          a(href='paramoji.js') Java Script
    div
      p
        | You can use the paramoji editor to find suitable parameters.
        | To really leverage the power of paramojis these parameters must of course be dynamically derived
        | or computed.
      +try-it


    h2.twocol Who is using paramojis?
    .twocol
      ul
        li <a href="https://cloudhiker.net/share/GM2GemeoHWEl">Cloudhiker</a> features Paramojis in the section "Fun Stuff"
        li <a href="https://www.continuousmoodmonitoring.org/en">Continuous Mood Monitoring:</a> A survery tool based on Paramoji emotions visualization
        li <a href="https://ranjithvenkatesh.com/2024/07/paramoji-speech-flow/">Paramoji Speech Flow</a> is a sentiment visualization of a speech text
        li <a href="https://juejin.cn/post/7169815266979741709#heading-7">JueJin</a> mentions Paramojis as a graphical resource
        li <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/InternetIsBeautiful/comments/ty7ip6/a_parametric_emoji_that_renders_facial/">Reddit</a> discusses Paramojis
    h2.twocol#references Are there any similar solutions?
    .twocol
      ul
        li
          | For a full parametric facial expression the best solution is still the
          | good old <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernoff_face">Chernoff face</a> from the 70's
        li
          | <a href="https://www.unige.ch/cisa/research/materials-and-online-research/facsgen/home/">FACSGen</a> - A facial expression generator tool based on the facial action coding system (FACS)
        li
          | <a href="https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Parametric-face-drawings%3A-A-demographically-diverse-Day-Davidenko/07c4a0dddf6903bb80b6f445185ecc9e58e978e9">Parametric
          | Face Drawings</a> is visualization technique for facial landmarks
        li
          | A research paper on
          | <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/44250675_Parametric_Facial_Expression_Synthesis_and_Animation">Parametric Facial Expression Synthesis</a>
        li
          | The <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/229589717_Parametric_Generation_of_Facial_Expressions_Based_on_FACS">Facial expression generator based on FACS</a>
          | is a pay-walled paper on facial expression generation.
        li
          | The <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235361517_A_Circumplex_Model_of_Affect"> Circumplex model of affect</a> uses two dimensions to define emotions
        li
          | The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PAD_emotional_state_model">PAD model</a> uses three dimensions to define emotions
        li
          | <a href="https://imotions.com/blog/learning/research-fundamentals/duchenne-smile/">The Many Faces of Emotions</a> dicusses the peculiarities of various emotions

